        <!-- MAIN CONTENT -->
        <section class="col-lg-9">

            <!-- PAGE TITLE -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h1 class="fw-bold">Agent Directive</h1>
                <p class="lead">
                    Learn the concepts, parameters, execution environments, and scoping rules required to allocate infrastructure nodes and containers within a declarative pipeline.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- INTRODUCTION -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">What is the Agent Directive?</h2>
                <p>
                    The <strong>agent</strong> directive is a mandatory structural element in a Declarative Jenkinsfile that dictates exactly where a pipeline, or a specific stage within it, will execute. It controls workspace allocation and instructs the central Jenkins controller on which worker node resources to provision for processing the pipeline steps.
                </p>

                <div class="alert alert-info my-3">
                    <strong>Simple Definition:</strong> The agent directive is a code statement that tells Jenkins which computer machine node, cluster group label, or isolated container type to use to run your build scripts.
                </div>

                <h3 class="h4 fw-bold mt-4">Supported Agent Allocation Options</h3>
                <p class="mb-2">Jenkins evaluates the agent parameter utilizing distinct structural modes to target execution nodes:</p>
                <ul class="mb-3">
                    <li class="mb-1"><strong>`agent any`:</strong> Instructs Jenkins to execute the pipeline or stage on any available and configured node across the cluster grid, regardless of its operating system type.</li>
                    <li class="mb-1"><strong>`agent none`:</strong> Disables global asset allocations at the root pipeline container level. Enforces the rule that every single nested <code>stage</code> must explicitly declare its own local execution agent parameter.</li>
                    <li class="mb-1"><strong>`agent { label 'mac-os-build' }`:</strong> Targets specific machines by forcing an exact match against configured label tags (e.g., restricting iOS app builds strictly to macOS agent systems).</li>
                    <li><strong>`agent { docker { image 'maven:3-alpine' } }`:</strong> Dynamically provisions an isolated Docker container context directly on a Docker-capable worker node, running all stage commands safely inside the container environment.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <!-- KEY CONCEPTS -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">Key Concepts</h2>
                <div class="row g-3">
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <h5>Global vs. Stage-Level Scope</h5>
                            <p class="mb-0">Understanding that placing the agent at the root level applies it globally to all stages, while defining a custom agent block inside a single stage overrides the global configuration for that specific task.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <h5>Workspace Environment Isolation</h5>
                            <p class="mb-0">Recognizing that switching agents between different pipeline stages forces Jenkins to generate entirely separate local workspaces, requiring file distributions to be managed via artifacts or stashing steps.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- EXAMPLE -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">Practical Jenkins Example</h2>
                <p>The following advanced blueprint demonstrates a multi-agent structure using `agent none` globally to distribute frontend and backend tasks to matching targeted environments:</p>
                <pre><code>pipeline {
    // 1. Disable global node binding to allow stage-level distribution
    agent none 


























    stages {
        stage('Compile Backend Assets') {
            // 2. Allocate an explicit Linux machine node matching a label
            agent { label 'ubuntu-worker-node' }
            steps {
                echo 'Executing Java compilation targets on an Ubuntu worker environment...'
                sh 'java -version'
            }
        }
        
        stage('Containerized Frontend Check') {
            // 3. Dynamically provision an isolated container for specific tools
            agent {
                docker {
                    image 'node:20-alpine'
                }
            }
            steps {
                echo 'Executing React testing suites inside an isolated Node container environment...'
                sh 'node --version'
                sh 'npm --version'
            }
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
                </div>

            <!-- PRACTICE -->
            <div class="mb-4">
        <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">Practice Exercise: Build a Distributed Agent Pipeline</h2>
                <div class="concept-box">
    <h5 class="fw-bold">Step-by-Step Task Sequence:</h5>
                    <ol class="ps-3 mb-0">
<li class="mb-2">Open the script block workspace of an active test pipeline configuration or launch a new Pipeline item from your dashboard interface.</li>
                        <li class="mb-2">Copy-paste the complete multi-agent code layout provided in the practical example section above into your configuration text terminal window.</li>
                        <li class="mb-2">If your environment does not have custom runner labels set up, adjust the first stage configuration line to read simply: `agent any`. Then click <strong>Save</strong>.</li>
<li class="mb-2">Trigger a manual validation run by selecting the **Build Now** menu action link on the left-side navigation portal.</li>
<li>Open the current build's **Console Output** screen to trace the log text lines, noting how Jenkins dynamically allocates resources and spins up container hooks for each stage.</li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>

<!-- SUMMARY -->
<div class="alert alert-info mt-4">
<h2 class="h4 fw-bold">Summary</h2>
<p class="mb-0">You have completed the <strong>Agent Directive</strong> lesson. Continue through the syllabus to learn how to organize execution flow steps cleanly by exploring the Stages and Steps lesson.</p>
</div>

